encyclopedia :the_three_forms do
  name "The Three Forms"
  summary "The Three Forms is a religion, cosmology, and working model that describes coherent resonance as paths closing into stable figures."
  kind :culture
  subkind :belief
  status :complete
  aka "the Trine", "the Triptych"
  topics :resonance, :cosmology, :religion
  prevalence :common
  available_globally

  prose <<~PROSE
    The Three Forms predates the band-and-bandwidth model the #{encyclopedia_ref :tuners, "Tuners"} use for #{encyclopedia_ref :resonance, "resonance"}. Keepers attribute its oldest teachings to the ring era and sometimes earlier. Its central claim is that a vibration traces a shape and tends toward a closed figure. The figure it closes into determines what it does.

    Most people meet the idea as a picture before they meet it as a theory. The Three Forms is drawn as a triptych — two figures of confident, lawful order flanking a third that the diagram itself marks as false. Tuners hang cheap printed versions over their benches. Scholars argue over the geometry. Devotional readers — the keepers of the #{ref :tessellan_communion, "Tessellan Communion"} chief among them — treat the three panels as a creed. The line written under every honest copy is the same: *Three forms, one conflict, one truth.*
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :how_it_works, heading: "The Closing of the Figure"
    A coherent resonance traces a path. The purer the coherence — the narrower the band, the truer the single wavelength — the more that path wants to return to where it began and close. A closed figure is stable: it braces itself, holds its frequency, and does reliable work. An open path bleeds, wanders, and fails. This is the whole of the model. Coherence is closure. To tune well is to help a vibration find its figure.

    Two figures dominate everything the system builds, because between them they cover what resonance is asked to do — to hold, and to move.

    **The Tetrahedron — the geometry of Form.** The figure of standing still: four faces bracing inward, the most stable close there is. This is the lawful shape of the **structural** band. When ringglass hardens a wall, the Three Forms says the stone is being coaxed toward the tetrahedral close — toward more form, more law, more of the quality the old panels label *light*, because a fully closed thing is visible and trustworthy. The Tetrahedron is gold in every drawing. It is the figure of the lattice, and the figure the ring itself was built to hold.

    **The Triangulum — the geometry of Will.** The figure of pushing: the smallest closed figure there is, and so the one that drives hardest. This is the lawful shape of the **kinetic** band — heat, motion, force. A #{encyclopedia_ref :kite_sail, "kite"} under thrust, a refinery running hot, a current of unopposed motion: all of it, in this model, is resonance closing into the triangle and leaning forward. The Triangulum is red, the color of the work it does. Where the Tetrahedron holds, the Triangulum moves. Between Form and Will, the keepers say, sits everything that can be honestly made.

    The **signal** band occupies the path between the two lawful figures, where a vibration is still resolving toward a close. The triptych leaves that path open. Keepers teach that a signal carried toward the single-wavelength horizon the #{encyclopedia_ref :elves, "elves"} pursued would resolve Form and Will into one perfectly closed figure, holding and moving without waste or bleed. They call this figure the One Truth. Living keepers preserve descriptions of it but no accepted drawing. Resonance scholars describe the same horizon as perfect coherence; the Three Forms treats it as a destination.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :the_third_panel, heading: "The Figure That Will Not Close"
    The third panel of every complete triptych is the #{ref :the_false_form, "False Form"}: an impossible rhombus whose edges meet in contradictory orientations, shimmering where the other two are solid. In the teaching, its path returns to its beginning while leaving the figure open.

    Workshop triptychs use the panel to show coherence through a violated close. In common speech the False Form names a lie that wears the shape of truth or the slow opening of a figure left untended. See the #{ref :the_false_form, "False Form"} for the figure of speech it has become.

    Communion keepers read the panel as a warning about order without truth. The two lawful figures can make a wall and an engine while leaving the system hollow; the One Truth is the complete figure toward which both reach. Some houses of the #{ref :tessellan_communion, "Tessellan Communion"} teach that the False Form exists only as a violated drawing. Others teach that a false closure can still exert force. Their disagreement determines whether the third figure may be sounded or only drawn.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :significance, heading: "Observance"
    How seriously the Three Forms are taken varies from person to person more than from place to place. Most people never think about the figures beyond the printed triptych on a workshop wall; the cosmology reaches them as vocabulary, not devotion. For the devout it is a religion in every ordinary sense — houses, study, obligations, and argument.

    Devotional practice centers on study circles. Participants trace the lattice figures by hand, recite the shared lines with their disputed variants named, and work through the closing sentence the way a court works through a precedent. Concealment and withdrawal run through the practice's oldest arguments: whether the One Truth withholds itself or remains unreached, and whether a figure left undrawn is protected or abandoned. The #{ref :tessellan_communion, "Tessellan Communion"} keeps the discipline's public form; household observance selects among the same readings and exercises.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :significance, heading: "A Question of Will"
    The tradition's oldest argument asks whether the Three Forms are principles or beings. Resonance scholarship reads them as models of how vibrations behave. Older devotional readings give the figures action: the Tetrahedron holds the world, the Triangulum drives it, and the False Form presses against both. Keepers preserve both readings in the disputed variants recited at study.

    Keepers also use the #{encyclopedia_ref :fae, "fae"} as a bodily example of closure. A fae concentrates into a clear figure and diffuses into an occupied haze when that effort fails. In Three Forms teaching, this is order held by will at the scale of one person.
  PROSE

  cue "Cheap printed triptychs hang over workshop benches, where a crew waiting on a repair can read the panels off the wall. Gold marks the structural band and red the kinetic, identifying what the machine in front of them does before its owner explains."
  affordance "Ask any working hand what a resonance device is doing and the answer comes back as figures closing rather than bands and " \
    "bandwidth, because the picture is what most people learned first. A #{encyclopedia_ref :tuners, "Clarisant-trained Tuner"} corrects the framing and then uses it anyway."
  pressure "When someone in a scene calls a thing a false form, the phrase can mean the drawn third panel or the ordinary word for a lie that holds its shape. Keepers hear the first and speakers usually intend the second, so an insult can land as a doctrinal claim."
  cue "A devotional circle sits tracing lattice figures by hand in careful silence, and the recitation that follows names its own disputed variants aloud, the way a court reads both precedents."
  variation "Some Communion houses teach that the False Form has no being of its own and may be freely drawn; others keep the same panel as a live warning and permit it drawn but never sounded."
  variation "Most people carry the cosmology as workshop vocabulary and a cheap triptych on the wall; the devout keep houses, study, obligations, and the old arguments about whether the figures choose."
end
